What was taken is an idea and a problem breakdown, not a body of code:

* a TTL-cached robots.txt store;
* a wildcard path-pattern translation for robots rules, and preservation of a
  bare ``?`` in such a rule;
* the shape of a per-domain rate limiter with escalating backoff;
* the split of those concerns into one small, dependency-light module.

Where those ideas live in the Crawl4AI tree — a reading pointer to the design,
**not** the source of any code here (these ranges were not copied):
``crawl4ai/utils.py:282-419`` (``RobotsParser``), ``crawl4ai/utils.py:54-82``,
``crawl4ai/utils.py:260-279`` (``_preserve_bare_query``) and
``crawl4ai/async_dispatcher.py:28-85`` (``RateLimiter``).

``politeness.py`` is a clean-room rewrite in this project's own synchronous,
stdlib-plus-httpx style.  No line of it was transcribed, translated or
mechanically adapted from a Crawl4AI source line.  Measured against
``crawl4ai/utils.py`` and ``crawl4ai/cache_validator.py``: the longest
contiguous span of code shared with Crawl4AI is **37 characters** — a plain
stdlib idiom (``PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL``, ``except Exception: return False``) —
and the longest run of identical source lines is **two trivial lines**.  The
shared code is negligible; what is attributed here is the design, which
copyright does not cover.  Under Apache-2.0 §4(b) this is also the notice that
the underlying design was modified: three defects of the original are fixed.

1. ``fetched_at`` is refreshed on every robots re-fetch, even when the body is
   byte-identical (the original skipped the write, so after the TTL expired it
   re-downloaded ``robots.txt`` on every call);
2. negative results (404 / 403 / 5xx / unparseable body) are cached;
3. ``Crawl-delay`` is parsed and honoured (the original never read it).

The rewrite also implements RFC 9309 matching (``*``, trailing ``$``, ``%2A`` /
``%24`` literals, merged user-agent groups, most-specific-match wins, Allow
beats Disallow on a tie) instead of ``urllib.robotparser``, which on Python
< 3.14 silently ignores wildcards. That difference is not academic in this
package: PyPI's robots.txt disallows ``/pypi/*/json`` and ``/pypi/*/*/json``,
and ``robotparser`` on Python 3.12 reads those patterns as literal prefixes and
reports the JSON API as *allowed*. The matcher in ``politeness.py`` blocks it —
see the "PyPI source" section of ``README.md``.

The GPL-3.0 part of Crawl4AI — the vendored ``crawl4ai/html2text/`` fork — was
**deliberately excluded**: no code, no data and no dependency from that tree is
used, shipped or installed by this package, and none ever should be.  This
module never converts HTML to markdown; that stays the caller's job
(``markdownify``).  Crawl4AI is **not** a dependency of this package; nothing
here imports it.
